Q1. What is the purpose of .gitignore?

Answer: The .gitignore file tells Git which untracked files and folders in a project to ignore so they are never staged or committed to the repository.

Easy way to remember: Think of it as a "Do Not Track" list for Git (useful for keeping temporary files, dependencies, and passwords out of your code history).

Q2. Pattern matching

Answer:

Plaintext
*.tmp
Easy way to remember: The * (asterisk) acts as a wildcard meaning "anything," so *.tmp matches any file name ending in .tmp.

Q3. Ignoring a specific file

Answer:

Plaintext
secrets.env
Easy way to remember: To ignore one exact file in your root folder, just type its exact filename.

Intermediate
Q4. Directory vs file patterns

Answer: Adding cache (without a slash) ignores both any file named cache and any folder named cache. Adding cache/ (with a trailing slash) tells Git to ignore only a directory (folder) named cache and everything inside it, while leaving any plain file named cache tracked.

Q5. The already-committed problem

Why does Git still track the file?
Because .gitignore only stops Git from tracking new, untracked files. Since config.env was already committed to the repository earlier, Git continues to watch it for changes.

What command stops Git from tracking it while keeping the file on disk?

Bash
git rm --cached config.env
(Note: The --cached flag removes the file from Git's tracking index without deleting the actual file from your computer).

Hard
Q6. Build a .gitignore from scratch

Answer:

Plaintext
*.pyc
__pycache__/
.venv/
.vscode/
.env
Q7. The timing mistake

Answer (copy and fill in):

Why this happens: .gitignore only prevents Git from tracking untracked files; if files were already staged or committed before .gitignore was created, Git continues tracking them.

Command to fix for a single file: git rm --cached <filename>

Best practice for next time: Always create and commit your .gitignore file right at the start of a project (immediately after git init) before adding or committing any other files.
